⭐️1. Python Distributors

■ Introduction

The first thing to know is that Python is distributed by various entities. 
This means there are several ways to install it, and your choice can affect how the system behaves or how you interact with Python.


■  Distributors

▷ CPython Release Team (python.org)
This is the most common official source. You can view all Python releases and download the installer for your Mac.

How: Download the .pkg file from the website and run the installer.

Where: /usr/local/bin/python3 (Default path)

Behavior: It installs a "framework" version of Python that is independent of the system.


▷ Homebrew (brew.sh)
Homebrew is a popular package manager that most macOS developers install. You can install Python by running the following command in your terminal:

brew install python

Where: /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 (on Apple Silicon Macs)

Behavior: When installed via Homebrew, the system will typically prioritize this version over others in your command line environment.

▷ Apple (System Python)
This version of Python comes pre-installed with macOS. We generally do not use this for development because Apple uses it for internal system processes.

Where: /usr/bin/python3


⭐️2. Locating Python Files

When you access your root directory (/), you may notice that folders like /usr/ are missing. This is because these system directories are hidden by default.


■ How to see hidden files:
In Finder: Press Cmd + Shift + . (period) to toggle hidden files on or off.

Go to Folder: Press Cmd + Shift + G in Finder and type the path (e.g., /usr/local/bin) to jump directly there.

In Browser: You can also browse your local file system by typing file:/// in your web browser's address bar.


⭐️3. Version Priority: Which Python is running?
What happens if you install Python using both Homebrew and the CPython installer?

By default, when you run the python3 command, macOS determines which one to use based on your PATH environment variable.


■ To check which Python is currently active, run: which python3

To use a specific version (e.g., the CPython version), you can call it by its full path: /usr/local/bin/python3

Understanding .zshrc
The .zshrc file (Z-shell Run Command) is a configuration script for the Zsh shell (the default terminal on macOS). Every time you open a new Terminal window, the system reads this file to set up your environment.

Usually, Homebrew adds a line like this to your .zshrc:
eval "$(/opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv)"

This ensures that the terminal looks into the Homebrew directory first. Without this configuration, the terminal might default to the older Apple-provided Python or fail to find your new installation. You can view the contents of this file by running:
cat ~/.zshrc



